1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
But a message recorded today could be decrypted later: harvest now, decrypt later.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
In August 2024, N I S T, the U S standards institute, published three post-quantum standards: one for keys, two for signatures.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The key one, M L Kem, rests on lattices: problems thought to resist quantum attacks.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Chrome and Firefox already mix it into their key exchange, alongside a classic elliptic curve.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
So strangers can agree on a key in public, because some sums are easy one way and hard the other.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
A lock can be public, if only its owner knows the trapdoor.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
And run backwards, the same maths signs.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Jevons was wrong about his number, but right that some sums are far easier done than undone.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Every secure connection begins that way: two strangers, in public, agreeing on a secret only they know.
